{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's defense fails to resolve the problem's core tension regarding 'acting on' a theory. While the target successfully frames corroboration as a methodological rule for maximizing falsification potential (learning), it does not explain why a scientist should *prefer* this theory for practical action or navigation of the future. Popper explicitly rejects the idea that past survival indicates future reliability (induction). If the preference is solely for 'efficient falsification,' then acting on the theory implies expecting it to hold long enough to be tested further, which smuggles in the very inductive assumption (that the future will resemble the past) that the target claims to avoid. The distinction between 'map for criticism' and 'basis for action' collapses when the agent must rely on the map to avoid immediate disaster; relying on it assumes its verisimilitude or stability, which is an inductive leap not justified by the 'informational content' argument alone.","premise":"Preferring a theory for action inherently requires an expectation of its future reliability, which constitutes an inductive assumption.","successor_question":"Can a Popperian justify acting on a corroborated theory without implicitly assuming that the conditions which allowed it to survive past tests will persist into the future?","target":"a768971d9cca81199fa3e89a20b60a938a224c90ba4994ba1a503d0fc1fcef5f"}]}